{"parentUuid":"c963a2db-48d4-4dda-a19c-267dabfdec17","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f021a654-6db4-4a07-a17f-44747f595730","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GEHXzPS4Uun5omvcxDbrtc","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"additions\":1004,\"author\":{\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzQx\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"Soph\",\"name\":\"Stefan Haubold\"},\"baseRefName\":\"main\",\"body\":\"## Add kubectl-style external commands (`entire-<name>` → `entire <name>`)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen a user types `entire command`, look for an `entire-command` binary on `$PATH` and exec it with the remaining args. Stdio, signals, and exit codes pass through. External commands have no protocol, no JSON contract — just argv in, exit code out. Built-ins always win on name conflicts; `entire-agent-*` is reserved for the [external agent protocol](docs/architecture/external-agent-protocol.md) and skipped here.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Why kubectl-style and not gh-style\\r\\n\\r\\ngh manages extensions through `gh extension install` and a dedicated install dir. That's heavier — version pinning, update notifications, an install registry. Kubectl-style PATH resolution is the lower-friction starting point and migrates cleanly to gh-style later if we ever want managed installs.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Design decisions worth reviewer attention\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Telemetry posture** — names on a hardcoded `officialPlugins` allowlist (currently empty) emit `cli_plugin_executed` events; everything else runs silently. Mirrors gh's policy because third-party command names can carry sensitive identifiers (project, vendor). Gated on exit-0 to match Cobra's `PersistentPostRun` semantics.\\r\\n- **Post-hook parity** — external commands bypass `rootCmd.ExecuteContext`, so `MaybeRunPlugin` runs `versioncheck.CheckAndNotify` itself. Without this, every external-command invocation would silently drop the version-update notice that built-ins receive.\\r\\n- **Non-executable detection** — `exec.LookPath` conflates \\\"not on PATH\\\" with \\\"found but not executable\\\". The resolver distinguishes them: a 0644 `entire-pgr` surfaces as a launch error, not a fall-through to \\\"unknown command\\\".\\r\\n- **Signal handling** — context cancellation sends `SIGINT` (with a 5s grace before SIGKILL via `cmd.WaitDelay`) so external commands can clean up rather than being force-killed.\\r\\n\\r\\n### What's not in this PR\\r\\n\\r\\n- No `entire plugin list` discovery command. Lazy resolution only.\\r\\n- `officialPlugins` is empty for now\\r\\n- No managed install dir.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Docs\\r\\n\\r\\n- New: [`docs/architecture/external-commands.md`](docs/architecture/external-commands.md) — resolution rules, author contract, telemetry posture, side-by-side comparison with the agent protocol\\r\\n- Updated: `CLAUDE.md` cross-link\\r\\n\\n<!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\\n---\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> **Medium Risk**\\n> Introduces a new pre-Cobra execution path in `main.go` that execs arbitrary PATH binaries and manages signals/env/exit codes; mistakes could change CLI routing or termination behavior.\\n> \\n> **Overview**\\n> Adds kubectl-style plugin dispatch: when `entire <name>` is not a built-in subcommand, the CLI now resolves and execs an `entire-<name>` binary on `$PATH` *before* Cobra runs, passing through stdio and propagating exit codes.\\n> \\n> Implements guardrails: built-ins always win, `agent-*`/`entire-agent-*` names are reserved and skipped, path traversal candidates are rejected, and found-but-non-executable plugins surface a clear launch error. Successful plugin runs mirror built-ins by running the CLI version-check notice and (only for allowlisted `officialPlugins`) emitting a new `cli_plugin_executed` telemetry event without recording plugin args/flags.\\n> \\n> Adds unit + integration coverage for dispatch behavior (args passthrough, env forwarding, exit code propagation, SIGINT delivery), exports `external.StripExeExt` for cross-platform reserved-name detection, and documents the feature in `docs/architecture/plugin-dispatch.md` (linked from `CLAUDE.md`).\\n> \\n> <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit ca83470908ec0b7e4bd64a396e060c5d5d573bb1. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup>\\n<!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->\",\"changedFiles\":13,\"deletions\":6,\"headRefName\":\"soph/external-command-support\",\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"External command support\"}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ef6fd3f3-64d3-4933-8a1f-af93728e2c95","timestamp":"2026-05-05T15:27:36.013Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"{\"additions\":1004,\"author\":{\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzQx\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"Soph\",\"name\":\"Stefan Haubold\"},\"baseRefName\":\"main\",\"body\":\"## Add kubectl-style external commands (`entire-<name>` → `entire <name>`)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen a user types `entire command`, look for an `entire-command` binary on `$PATH` and exec it with the remaining args. Stdio, signals, and exit codes pass through. External commands have no protocol, no JSON contract — just argv in, exit code out. Built-ins always win on name conflicts; `entire-agent-*` is reserved for the [external agent protocol](docs/architecture/external-agent-protocol.md) and skipped here.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Why kubectl-style and not gh-style\\r\\n\\r\\ngh manages extensions through `gh extension install` and a dedicated install dir. That's heavier — version pinning, update notifications, an install registry. Kubectl-style PATH resolution is the lower-friction starting point and migrates cleanly to gh-style later if we ever want managed installs.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Design decisions worth reviewer attention\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Telemetry posture** — names on a hardcoded `officialPlugins` allowlist (currently empty) emit `cli_plugin_executed` events; everything else runs silently. Mirrors gh's policy because third-party command names can carry sensitive identifiers (project, vendor). Gated on exit-0 to match Cobra's `PersistentPostRun` semantics.\\r\\n- 