tially better\n\n- **Visibility/management** — `list`, `remove`, eventual `upgrade`/`pin` give users (and us) a way to enumerate and control what's installed. PATH is opaque.\n- **Eventual binary release-asset install + manifest** — automatic `entire plugin install owner/repo` UX. Soph's PR punts entirely to \"drop the binary on PATH yourself.\"\n- **Per-plugin data dir** for durable plugin state.\n- **An exec escape hatch** for built-in shadowing. Soph's design has no recovery once a built-in name is taken.\n\n## Recommended path forward\n\nThe two designs answer different questions, and Soph's is the right first step. The plan doc itself says kubectl-style \"migrates cleanly to gh-style later if we ever want managed installs\" — that's exactly what should happen.\n\nConcretely:\n\n1. **Land #1104 as the dispatcher.** Pre-Cobra resolution, SIGINT handling, agent reservation, env contract, telemetry posture, integration tests, docs are all wins to take wholesale.\n2. **Close #1116 (or repurpose it as a follow-up branch).** The two main.go entry points conflict, and shipping both creates two parallel resolution paths users have to reason about.\n3. **Cherry-pick from #1116 into the follow-up only what's actually needed:**\n   - `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` env var so plugins have a sanctioned place to write state regardless of where the binary lives.\n   - A future `entire plugin install/list/remove` group that writes to a managed dir which is *also* discovered by the kubectl resolver — managed install becomes additive, not a parallel mechanism.\n   - The `plugin exec <name>` escape hatch when built-in shadowing becomes a real problem.\n   - Stricter name validation (`ValidName`) on top of the existing path-traversal rejection.\n4. **Defer the rest of #1116's plan** (release-asset install, git-clone scripts, pinning, upgrade, search/browse, create scaffolds) until there's evidence users actually want managed installs over \"drop a binary on PATH.\"\n\nIf you want, I can 